B. Actual US shares

That's a completely different problem.

We cannot simply create an AAPL token and claim that it represents actual Apple shares.

Robinhood's own Stock Tokens are specifically tokenized debt securities issued by Robinhood Assets (Jersey) Limited, rather than direct legal ownership of the underlying shares.

And their documentation explicitly says developers compose with existing Stock Tokens rather than minting them themselves.

So we'd use the existing permitted RWA tokens rather than attempting to create our own Apple/Nvidia securities.

The biggest problem isn't technical

It's regulation + custody + who can invest.

This is particularly important if the idea is:

"Anyone deposits $100 and our trader/AI manages it."

That starts looking very different legally from simply building a DeFi application.

Robinhood's Stock Token documentation itself places substantial geographic restrictions on the underlying products. For example, Stock Tokens aren't available to U.S. persons and have restrictions in several other jurisdictions.

And Robinhood's own tokenization memo says that under the current U.S. framework, the vast majority of tokenized RWAs are treated as securities.

So the smart contract is absolutely buildable.

The legal wrapper around a publicly offered managed investment product is the difficult part.

